WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.587
When an agency retires Active Directory,

2
00:00:02.587 --> 00:00:07.587
two questions come up that nobody owned
before, because AD answered them quietly.

3
00:00:07.587 --> 00:00:09.300
Who gives a device its name?

4
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.718
And who puts that name into
DNS, the system that turns

5
00:00:12.718 --> 00:00:16.612
names into network addresses,
so other machines can find it?

6
00:00:16.612 --> 00:00:18.563
This explainer walks through both.

7
00:00:18.563 --> 00:00:20.735
It's built for someone still learning,

8
00:00:20.735 --> 00:00:23.750
so every term is explained
the first time it appears.

9
00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:26.438
We start with the two jobs AD did at once.

10
00:00:26.438 --> 00:00:28.813
Then who names each kind of device now.

11
00:00:28.813 --> 00:00:33.319
Then why devices can't register
their own names securely without AD,

12
00:00:33.319 --> 00:00:37.987
what takes over, and where nothing
does, such as IPv6 and remote clients.

13
00:00:37.987 --> 00:00:42.728
Then why names still matter, and a
checklist of what the agency must do,

14
00:00:42.728 --> 00:00:44.974
before, during and after cutover.

15
00:00:44.974 --> 00:00:46.775
This is research under review.

16
00:00:46.775 --> 00:00:51.950
Every fact comes from sources opened
on October twenty-second, 2026.

17
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:56.750
Where I drew a conclusion that no source
states directly, I'll call it an inference.

18
00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:01.725
And recommendations are labeled program
design guidance, not vendor requirements.

19
00:01:01.725 --> 00:01:08.646
Sources: Research brief, After
Active Directory: Who Names Devices,

20
00:01:08.646 --> 00:01:16.070
Who Registers Their DNS Records, and
What an Agency Must Do (verified Oct.

21
00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:23.872
22, 2026); Microsoft Learn, Apple,
freedesktop.org, cloud-init, NetworkManager,

22
00:01:23.872 --> 00:01:29.912
FreeIPA, AWS and Infoblox NIOS
9.1 documentation; IETF RFCs;

23
00:01:29.912 --> 00:01:37.810
the Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 05 Ch04; Book 08 Ch04, Ch07, Ch10.

24
00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.440
In Active Directory,
a Windows PC joined to

25
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.485
the domain gets two jobs
done almost for free.

26
00:01:44.485 --> 00:01:45.835
Job one is the name.

27
00:01:45.835 --> 00:01:49.517
The PC has a computer name,
say pc-zero-zero-four-two,

28
00:01:49.517 --> 00:01:55.085
and a primary DNS suffix, which is usually
the name of the AD domain it joined.

29
00:01:55.085 --> 00:01:57.948
Put them together and
you get its full name,

30
00:01:57.948 --> 00:02:00.647
its FQDN, or fully qualified domain name.

31
00:02:00.647 --> 00:02:03.172
Job two is the DNS record.

32
00:02:03.172 --> 00:02:06.293
An A record maps a name
to an IPv4 address;

33
00:02:06.293 --> 00:02:12.343
an AAAA record maps a name to an IPv6
address; and a PTR record goes the other way,

34
00:02:12.343 --> 00:02:15.935
address to name, in what's
called a reverse zone.

35
00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:20.823
Dynamic update is the standard way to add
or change those records over the network.

36
00:02:20.823 --> 00:02:26.141
In AD, the domain's DNS zone lives in
the directory, on domain controllers,

37
00:02:26.141 --> 00:02:30.197
and by default it accepts
only secure dynamic updates.

38
00:02:30.197 --> 00:02:35.735
Secure means the update is signed with
Kerberos, AD's ticket-based sign-in protocol,

39
00:02:35.735 --> 00:02:39.073
and once a name exists, only
its owner can change it.

40
00:02:39.073 --> 00:02:43.422
The domain-joined PC has a machine
account in AD, so it can sign.

41
00:02:43.422 --> 00:02:46.724
By default, the PC
registers its own A record

42
00:02:46.724 --> 00:02:50.209
and the Windows DHCP
server registers the PTR.

43
00:02:50.209 --> 00:02:55.185
When AD retires, both jobs need a new
owner, and the second one is harder.

44
00:02:55.185 --> 00:03:01.344
Sources: the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08, Chapter 04,

45
00:03:01.344 --> 00:03:09.099
What AD-integrated DNS actually is, and
Secure dynamic updates and the DHCP server;

46
00:03:09.099 --> 00:03:14.346
Microsoft Learn, Dynamic update
(Windows Server DNS) [S26];

47
00:03:14.346 --> 00:03:17.550
Policy CSP ADMX_DnsClient [S27].

48
00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:19.975
So who names the device now?

49
00:03:19.975 --> 00:03:23.263
The short answer: whatever
provisions it, at build time.

50
00:03:23.263 --> 00:03:28.338
For Windows, that's usually Autopilot,
which sets up a new PC out of the box.

51
00:03:28.338 --> 00:03:31.398
Classic Autopilot with
Microsoft Entra join,

52
00:03:31.398 --> 00:03:36.229
which joins the PC to Microsoft's cloud
directory instead of AD, has a name

53
00:03:36.229 --> 00:03:38.806
template: fifteen characters or fewer,

54
00:03:38.806 --> 00:03:42.188
with macros for the serial
number or random digits.

55
00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:45.588
Hybrid join, which
joins both AD and Entra,

56
00:03:45.588 --> 00:03:48.888
gets only a prefix,
and the rest is random.

57
00:03:48.888 --> 00:03:53.332
The newer Autopilot device preparation
now has a name template too,

58
00:03:53.332 --> 00:03:55.237
up to sixty-three characters.

59
00:03:55.237 --> 00:03:59.507
The caveat matters: it works only
for devices with device association,

60
00:03:59.507 --> 00:04:03.925
and has no effect on devices onboarded
with corporate identifiers only.

61
00:04:03.925 --> 00:04:07.961
As an inference, the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 05's

62
00:04:07.961 --> 00:04:12.075
description of device preparation
needs a review against this.

63
00:04:12.075 --> 00:04:16.908
After enrollment, Intune's Rename action
can rename corporate-owned Windows

64
00:04:16.908 --> 00:04:21.212
PCs and Macs, but not hybrid-joined
devices, which need domain methods.

65
00:04:21.212 --> 00:04:24.604
Macs have no enrollment-time
name template that I found;

66
00:04:24.604 --> 00:04:26.374
you rename after enrollment.

67
00:04:26.374 --> 00:04:29.837
Linux takes its name from
cloud-init or a static hostname.

68
00:04:29.837 --> 00:04:33.813
Azure Arc keeps the server's
hostname as a fixed resource name.

69
00:04:33.813 --> 00:04:36.362
Cloud VMs get names at create time.

70
00:04:36.362 --> 00:04:43.943
Sources: Microsoft Learn: Autopilot device
preparation Entra join policy [S1],

71
00:04:43.943 --> 00:04:51.643
Compare device preparation and Autopilot
[S2], Entra join Autopilot profile [S3],

72
00:04:51.643 --> 00:04:57.803
Hybrid join Domain Join profile
[S4], Rename a device in Intune [S5],

73
00:04:57.803 --> 00:05:03.134
Accounts CSP [S6], macOS
automated device enrollment [S7];

74
00:05:03.134 --> 00:05:09.294
hostnamectl and hostname(5) [S10,
S11]; cloud-init Set Hostname [S12];

75
00:05:09.294 --> 00:05:15.691
azcmagent connect and Arc rename
[S15, S16]; Azure ComputerName [S17];

76
00:05:15.691 --> 00:05:20.210
EC2 hostname types [S21];
Book 05 Ch04, Naming.

77
00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:22.222
Here's the core problem.

78
00:05:22.222 --> 00:05:28.472
A PC joined only to Microsoft Entra ID
still tries to register its name in DNS.

79
00:05:28.472 --> 00:05:29.484
What happens?

80
00:05:29.484 --> 00:05:32.840
By default, Windows tries
an unsigned update first,

81
00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:36.035
and only if that's refused
does it try a secure one.

82
00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:39.039
A zone that is set to
accept only secure updates

83
00:05:39.039 --> 00:05:41.735
refuses the unsigned
attempt, as it should.

84
00:05:41.735 --> 00:05:46.191
Then the secure attempt: Microsoft
documents that Windows dynamic

85
00:05:46.191 --> 00:05:50.885
update clients and DNS servers can
use only Kerberos for secure update.

86
00:05:50.885 --> 00:05:55.320
As an inference, an Entra-joined PC
has no machine account in the domain,

87
00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:59.373
so it can't sign, and the zone gets
no record from that client at all.

88
00:05:59.373 --> 00:06:03.084
The tempting fix is to let the
zone accept unsigned updates.

89
00:06:03.084 --> 00:06:03.773
Don't.

90
00:06:03.773 --> 00:06:06.404
Microsoft says that without secure update,

91
00:06:06.404 --> 00:06:10.059
any computer on the network can
modify the existing A record.

92
00:06:10.059 --> 00:06:11.135
More facts.

93
00:06:11.135 --> 00:06:15.447
Over a VPN, Windows doesn't
attempt dynamic update by default.

94
00:06:15.447 --> 00:06:21.130
Since the May 2020 Windows 10 update, it
also skips servers named in an NRPT rule,

95
00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:27.161
the Name Resolution Policy Table, which
sends certain names to certain DNS servers.

96
00:06:27.161 --> 00:06:29.939
And Microsoft's advice
to let clients register

97
00:06:29.939 --> 00:06:32.860
their own records assumes
domain-joined clients.

98
00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:35.222
As an inference, it can't apply here.

99
00:06:35.222 --> 00:06:38.834
So something else has to register
endpoints, and it has to sign.

100
00:06:38.834 --> 00:06:45.587
Sources: Microsoft Learn: Dynamic
update (Windows Server DNS) [S26];

101
00:06:45.587 --> 00:06:48.772
Policy CSP ADMX_DnsClient [S27];

102
00:06:48.772 --> 00:06:57.309
How to enable or disable DNS updates in
Windows [S28]; Troubleshooting DNS dynamic

103
00:06:57.309 --> 00:07:05.710
update issues [S29]; DirectAccess known
issues (DNS registration and NRPT) [S56].

104
00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:09.047
What takes over is the DHCP server.

105
00:07:09.047 --> 00:07:14.848
DHCP is the service that hands a device
its address for a set time, called a lease.

106
00:07:14.848 --> 00:07:18.636
It already talks to every device,
so it can register names for them.

107
00:07:18.636 --> 00:07:19.722
Here's the flow.

108
00:07:19.722 --> 00:07:25.049
The device asks for an address and sends
its host name, in DHCP option twelve,

109
00:07:25.049 --> 00:07:29.259
or its full name in option
eighty-one, the client FQDN option.

110
00:07:29.259 --> 00:07:34.285
For IPv6 the equivalent is
DHCPv6 option thirty-nine.

111
00:07:34.285 --> 00:07:40.586
The DHCP server is set to always update
DNS, and signs its updates with TSIG,

112
00:07:40.586 --> 00:07:45.235
a shared-key signature, or
GSS-TSIG, which uses Kerberos.

113
00:07:45.235 --> 00:07:48.636
On Infoblox NIOS, a DDI
platform, meaning DNS,

114
00:07:48.636 --> 00:07:54.400
DHCP and IP address management in one
product, a server accepts no dynamic

115
00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:59.785
updates by default; a setting called
Allow updates from lists who may.

116
00:07:59.785 --> 00:08:02.210
Make that the DHCP servers only.

117
00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:03.460
Two safeguards.

118
00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:07.359
Beside each name, the server
writes an ownership record,

119
00:08:07.359 --> 00:08:10.072
so only one client holds a name at a time.

120
00:08:10.072 --> 00:08:13.930
That's a TXT record in
Infoblox's default Interim mode,

121
00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:16.385
or a DHCID record in Standard mode.

122
00:08:16.385 --> 00:08:20.510
And when the lease expires, the server
removes the records it created.

123
00:08:20.510 --> 00:08:26.398
On Windows DHCP, always updating both records
makes the client skip its own attempt.

124
00:08:26.398 --> 00:08:32.641
Sources: RFC 4701 DHCID, RFC
4702 DHCPv4 Client FQDN option,

125
00:08:32.641 --> 00:08:40.965
RFC 4703 FQDN conflict resolution, RFC
4704 DHCPv6 Client FQDN option [S31–S34];

126
00:08:40.965 --> 00:08:46.689
Microsoft Learn Dynamic update
[S26]; Infoblox NIOS 9.1:

127
00:08:46.689 --> 00:08:53.973
Understanding DDNS Updates from
DHCP, About the Client FQDN Option,

128
00:08:53.973 --> 00:08:59.696
Sending Updates for Zones
on an External Name Server,

129
00:08:59.696 --> 00:09:06.330
Configuring DNS Servers for
DDNS, Configuring DDNS Features,

130
00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:13.354
Accepting DDNS Updates from DHCP
Clients [S40–S44, S47]; the Federal

131
00:09:13.354 --> 00:09:21.590
Modernization Workbook's Book 08 Ch04,
Secure dynamic updates and the DHCP server.

132
00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:23.840
Now the migration trap.

133
00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:28.090
Ownership records only help if the
old and new systems agree on them.

134
00:09:28.090 --> 00:09:33.165
Infoblox's default mode, Interim,
checks ownership with TXT records.

135
00:09:33.165 --> 00:09:38.253
Its Standard mode uses DHCID records,
the format the standards define.

136
00:09:38.253 --> 00:09:42.290
Infoblox documents three cases
where TXT checking fails.

137
00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:47.744
First, migration from Microsoft-based
systems: those records have no TXT,

138
00:09:47.744 --> 00:09:52.653
and, in Infoblox's words, new DDNS
updates fail after the migration.

139
00:09:52.653 --> 00:09:57.028
Second, a mix of Microsoft
and ISC DHCP servers.

140
00:09:57.028 --> 00:09:59.862
Third, mobility: a
laptop's wired and Wi-Fi

141
00:09:59.862 --> 00:10:03.040
interfaces look like
two different clients.

142
00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:05.165
The second trap is on Macs.

143
00:10:05.165 --> 00:10:08.325
Apple's private Wi-Fi
address feature gives a Mac a

144
00:10:08.325 --> 00:10:12.178
different hardware address, its
MAC address, on each network.

145
00:10:12.178 --> 00:10:15.774
Fixed is the default on
WPA2 or better networks;

146
00:10:15.774 --> 00:10:19.278
Rotating changes the
address every two weeks.

147
00:10:19.278 --> 00:10:23.728
An MDM, mobile device management,
profile can turn it off with a key

148
00:10:23.728 --> 00:10:28.839
called DisableAssociationMACRandomization,
on macOS fifteen and later.

149
00:10:28.839 --> 00:10:33.129
As an inference, a rotating
address changes the DHCP identity

150
00:10:33.129 --> 00:10:37.165
that ownership records rest
on, the same mobility failure.

151
00:10:37.165 --> 00:10:40.415
I didn't verify the Android
or Windows equivalents.

152
00:10:40.415 --> 00:10:44.435
The program design guidance:
pick Standard or Interim once,

153
00:10:44.435 --> 00:10:49.440
migrate deliberately, and turn off
address randomization on agency Wi-Fi.

154
00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:51.927
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1:

155
00:10:51.927 --> 00:10:58.678
Enabling DDNS for IPv4 and IPv6 DHCP
Clients (Interim vs Standard) [S45],

156
00:10:58.678 --> 00:11:04.364
Configuring DDNS Update
Verification [S46]; Apple Support,

157
00:11:04.364 --> 00:11:09.101
Use private Wi-Fi addresses
on Apple devices [S49];

158
00:11:09.101 --> 00:11:14.668
apple/device-management
com.apple.wifi.managed.yaml [S50];

159
00:11:14.668 --> 00:11:20.720
research checklist steps 6 and
7 (program design guidance).

160
00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:24.807
There are two ways a device
gets an IPv6 address.

161
00:11:24.807 --> 00:11:28.308
In the first, a DHCPv6 server assigns it.

162
00:11:28.308 --> 00:11:30.037
The server knows the address,

163
00:11:30.037 --> 00:11:33.496
so it can register an AAAA
record using option thirty-nine.

164
00:11:33.496 --> 00:11:37.746
In the second, called SLAAC,
stateless address autoconfiguration,

165
00:11:37.746 --> 00:11:41.920
the device builds its own address
from what the router announces.

166
00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:44.905
Devices also create temporary, or privacy,

167
00:11:44.905 --> 00:11:48.657
addresses, typically used
for outgoing connections.

168
00:11:48.657 --> 00:11:54.346
The DHCPv6 name option covers
only addresses DHCPv6 assigned.

169
00:11:54.346 --> 00:11:59.160
So, as an inference, no DHCPv6
server ever learns a SLAAC address,

170
00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:03.882
and no server-side registration
creates an AAAA record for it.

171
00:12:03.882 --> 00:12:07.318
The device could register
it itself, but as we saw,

172
00:12:07.318 --> 00:12:10.582
a post-AD Windows client
can't do that securely.

173
00:12:10.582 --> 00:12:15.103
The consequence, also an inference:
for Entra-joined endpoints,

174
00:12:15.103 --> 00:12:20.145
treat AAAA records for SLAAC or
privacy addresses as absent by design.

175
00:12:20.145 --> 00:12:24.201
Find endpoints by their
DHCP-assigned addresses,

176
00:12:24.201 --> 00:12:29.445
and correlate IPv6 activity from
DHCP and neighbor logs, not DNS.

177
00:12:29.445 --> 00:12:35.797
Two cloud notes: in AWS, only the instance
resource name returns AAAA records,

178
00:12:35.797 --> 00:12:41.645
and Azure's DNS Private Resolver
doesn't support IPv6-enabled subnets.

179
00:12:41.645 --> 00:12:45.720
And I couldn't verify whether Windows
registers its temporary addresses.

180
00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:52.553
Sources: RFC 4862 IPv6 Stateless
Address Autoconfiguration [S37];

181
00:12:52.553 --> 00:12:57.108
RFC 8981 Temporary
Address Extensions [S38];

182
00:12:57.108 --> 00:13:05.332
RFC 4704 DHCPv6 Client FQDN option [S34];
Amazon EC2 instance hostname types [S21];

183
00:13:05.332 --> 00:13:13.557
the Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 08 Ch07 (Azure DNS Private Resolver

184
00:13:13.557 --> 00:13:17.606
platform constraints); research brief,

185
00:13:17.606 --> 00:13:23.280
Couldn't verify (Windows
temporary IPv6 addresses).

186
00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:27.555
Servers and cloud machines don't
follow the endpoint pattern.

187
00:13:27.555 --> 00:13:30.316
On premises, servers with fixed addresses

188
00:13:30.316 --> 00:13:33.393
get static records in
the IPAM and DDI system.

189
00:13:33.393 --> 00:13:36.275
Static records aren't
removed by scavenging,

190
00:13:36.275 --> 00:13:39.080
the cleanup job that
deletes stale entries.

191
00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:42.950
As program design guidance,
the build pipeline that creates

192
00:13:42.950 --> 00:13:46.742
a server should also create and
delete its A and PTR records.

193
00:13:46.742 --> 00:13:51.670
Linux servers in an identity domain
can register themselves with GSS-TSIG,

194
00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:56.041
but watch for one documented
behavior: if signing fails at install,

195
00:13:56.041 --> 00:13:59.379
the client falls back to
unauthenticated updates.

196
00:13:59.379 --> 00:14:02.941
For Azure Arc, set the
resource name to match the DNS

197
00:14:02.941 --> 00:14:06.417
name at connect time, because
it can't change later.

198
00:14:06.417 --> 00:14:11.091
In Azure, private DNS autoregistration
does the job, with limits:

199
00:14:11.091 --> 00:14:16.469
virtual machines only, the primary
network interface only, A records only,

200
00:14:16.469 --> 00:14:21.318
no PTR records, and one autoregistration
zone per virtual network.

201
00:14:21.318 --> 00:14:24.583
Records disappear when the
VM is deleted or stopped,

202
00:14:24.583 --> 00:14:29.217
deleted records can't be restored, and
unlinking the network deletes them.

203
00:14:29.217 --> 00:14:33.242
In AWS, instances get AWS-made names.

204
00:14:33.242 --> 00:14:38.919
An AWS blog from 2016 says VPC DNS
doesn't create records dynamically,

205
00:14:38.919 --> 00:14:42.405
and offers a sample event-driven pipeline.

206
00:14:42.405 --> 00:14:48.630
I found no built-in registration into
your own Route 53 zones in AWS's docs.

207
00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:56.360
