213
00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:34.927
What about people working remotely?

214
00:15:34.927 --> 00:15:39.940
Take Microsoft's Global Secure Access,
which includes Entra Private Access.

215
00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:42.610
You list your private DNS suffixes,

216
00:15:42.610 --> 00:15:46.752
and its client on the laptop
turns them into NRPT rules.

217
00:15:46.752 --> 00:15:50.530
Matching queries go to a DNS
proxy at Microsoft's edge,

218
00:15:50.530 --> 00:15:55.853
and a connector inside your network
resolves them against on-premises DNS.

219
00:15:55.853 --> 00:15:58.513
The laptop gets back a
synthetic IP address,

220
00:15:58.513 --> 00:16:01.027
a stand-in that routes through the tunnel.

221
00:16:01.027 --> 00:16:04.878
Notice what's missing: that's all
about the laptop finding names.

222
00:16:04.878 --> 00:16:09.013
I found no primary source
saying Global Secure Access

223
00:16:09.013 --> 00:16:13.240
registers the laptop's own
name or address in agency DNS.

224
00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:18.840
So, as an inference, remote clients are
resolvers of names, not registrants.

225
00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:22.740
Windows also skips dynamic
update over VPN by default.

226
00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:25.615
Third-party VPN products
weren't researched.

227
00:16:25.615 --> 00:16:28.890
That changes how the help
desk finds a remote device.

228
00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:32.559
Intune's Remote Help starts
from the Intune admin center:

229
00:16:32.559 --> 00:16:36.853
you pick the device from the device
list, and no DNS lookup is needed.

230
00:16:36.853 --> 00:16:39.140
But you pick it by its Intune device name.

231
00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:42.803
As an inference, that name
must be unique and meaningful.

232
00:16:42.803 --> 00:16:46.147
One subtlety: renaming in the
admin center doesn't change

233
00:16:46.147 --> 00:16:49.491
the management name or the name
in the Company Portal app.

234
00:16:49.491 --> 00:16:57.201
Sources: Microsoft Learn: Understand
Microsoft Entra Private DNS and Troubleshoot

235
00:16:57.201 --> 00:17:03.150
application access (Global Secure
Access) [S51]; Dynamic update [S26];

236
00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:10.201
DirectAccess known issues (NRPT) [S56];
Using Remote Help with Microsoft Intune

237
00:17:10.201 --> 00:17:16.950
[S52]; TeamViewer integration in Intune
[S53]; Rename a device in Intune [S5].

238
00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:21.700
If devices sign in with cloud
tokens, why do names still matter?

239
00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:22.725
Four reasons.

240
00:17:22.725 --> 00:17:24.713
First, Kerberos islands.

241
00:17:24.713 --> 00:17:29.859
Microsoft says an Entra-joined device
signing in to on-premises resources needs

242
00:17:29.859 --> 00:17:35.237
line-of-sight to domain controllers, which
clients find through DNS service records.

243
00:17:35.237 --> 00:17:40.401
And the Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 08 explains that if a name resolves to

244
00:17:40.401 --> 00:17:42.983
the wrong host, a
perfectly valid ticket is

245
00:17:42.983 --> 00:17:45.712
presented to the wrong
machine and rejected.

246
00:17:45.712 --> 00:17:47.474
Second, certificates.

247
00:17:47.474 --> 00:17:50.025
A certificate binds a name to a key.

248
00:17:50.025 --> 00:17:52.434
Intune's SCEP certificate profiles,

249
00:17:52.434 --> 00:17:57.573
the protocol Intune uses to request
device certificates, offer a device-name

250
00:17:57.573 --> 00:18:00.464
variable, but the full
domain name variable

251
00:18:00.464 --> 00:18:03.275
is only for Windows domain-joined devices.

252
00:18:03.275 --> 00:18:09.131
As an inference, after AD the certificate's
DNS name has to be built from the device

253
00:18:09.131 --> 00:18:11.404
name plus a fixed agency suffix,

254
00:18:11.404 --> 00:18:15.337
and it only matches DNS if
that same name is registered.

255
00:18:15.337 --> 00:18:16.613
Third, logs.

256
00:18:16.613 --> 00:18:21.700
Book 12 joins every evidence record on
an asset identity from the naming plane.

257
00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:26.913
Microsoft's own log model calls a hostname
used as a device ID a last resort.

258
00:18:26.913 --> 00:18:31.525
As an inference, an unregistered or
duplicated name breaks that join.

259
00:18:31.525 --> 00:18:37.387
Fourth, duplicates: Microsoft says its
naming macros don't guarantee unique names.

260
00:18:37.387 --> 00:18:46.321
Sources: Microsoft Learn: How SSO to
on-premises resources works on Microsoft Entra

261
00:18:46.321 --> 00:18:54.736
joined devices [S55]; Create and assign
SCEP certificate profiles in Intune [S54];

262
00:18:54.736 --> 00:19:01.728
Accounts CSP [S6]; Infoblox NIOS 9.1
About the Client FQDN Option [S41];

263
00:19:01.728 --> 00:19:09.496
Book 08 Ch04 (What Kerberos needs from
DNS); Book 12 Ch02 (evidence contract,

264
00:19:09.496 --> 00:19:14.640
ASIM); Book 15 Ch07 (CMDB
reconcile); Book 07 Ch01.

265
00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:16.652
Here's the whole picture.

266
00:19:16.652 --> 00:19:19.764
The full version, with
notes, is in the backup.

267
00:19:19.764 --> 00:19:24.987
For Windows devices joined to Entra,
Autopilot or Intune names the device,

268
00:19:24.987 --> 00:19:28.753
and the DHCP server registers
it, with signed updates.

269
00:19:28.753 --> 00:19:33.782
Hybrid-joined Windows, during the
transition, keeps working as it does today,

270
00:19:33.782 --> 00:19:37.215
through AD; it moves to the
first row when it's rebuilt.

271
00:19:37.215 --> 00:19:40.128
Macs are renamed in
Intune after enrollment.

272
00:19:40.128 --> 00:19:44.281
The DHCP server should register
them, but that's an inference:

273
00:19:44.281 --> 00:19:47.853
I couldn't verify how macOS
behaves with dynamic DNS.

274
00:19:47.853 --> 00:19:52.873
Linux gets its name from cloud-init or a
static hostname, and DHCP registers it,

275
00:19:52.873 --> 00:19:56.716
unless it's in an identity domain
that signs its own updates.

276
00:19:56.716 --> 00:20:00.590
On-premises servers with fixed
addresses get static records.

277
00:20:00.590 --> 00:20:05.790
Azure VMs are named at create time and
autoregistered, within the limits we saw.

278
00:20:05.790 --> 00:20:07.905
AWS instances get AWS names,

279
00:20:07.905 --> 00:20:13.053
and anything custom needs your
own pipeline or IPAM integration.

280
00:20:13.053 --> 00:20:17.397
And printers and other devices that
can't be managed get their names from

281
00:20:17.397 --> 00:20:21.814
a reservation or the device itself,
with DHCP registering on their behalf.

282
00:20:21.814 --> 00:20:31.930
Sources: Research brief, Per-platform
table, and the sources cited in each row:

283
00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:41.243
